is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The FRRouting (FRR) control-plane on router1, specifically bgpd (and the frr service overall) is down.
- Mechanism: With BGP not running on router1, no route to the partner network 200.1.1.0/24 is learned or installed. Traffic from pc1 to pc2 hits router1 and is rejected with ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable.” On the far side, router2 also lacks a route to 195.11.14.0/24 and returns the same. This matches the observed bidirectional failures.
- Localization: 
  • Physical link router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0 is up on both ends (ethtool shows “Link detected: yes”). 
  • router1 FRR: “frr.service … Active: failed (failed)” and “show bgp summary: bgpd is not running.” router1’s routing table contains only connected routes (193.10.11.0/24, 195.11.14.0/24). 
  • router2 FRR is running; BGP neighbor 193.10.11.1 is stuck in Active, confirming the peer (router1) isn’t responding.
- Root cause: The FRR service (bgpd) on router1 is stopped/failed, leaving BGP down and no inter-AS routes.
- Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router1. Action: systemctl restart frr on router1. Then verify “show bgp summary” shows the session Established with neighbor 193.10.11.1 and that routes to 200.1.1.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24 appear on both routers. No recabling is needed; the inter-AS link is healthy. If FRR fails again, inspect /etc/frr/frr.conf and logs (/var/log/frr/frr.log) for configuration or syntax errors before re-enabling the service.
=== END ===